<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zoltán Kodály</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kodály Zoltán</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zoltán Kodály was a Hungarian composer, ethnomusicologist and music educator, born in Kecskemét, known for the Kodály method of music teaching. From 1905 he recorded folk songs in remote villages on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phonograph">phonograph</a> cylinders and introduced his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-bartok">Béla Bartók</a> to folk-song collecting. His works blend Western European styles with Hungarian folk music; his first major success was the Psalmus Hungaricus in 1923, followed by Háry János (1926) and the Dances of Galánta (1933). From 1935 he worked to reform music teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s schools, and his principles, built on singing and on folk songs in the pupils' mother tongue, spread after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zoltan-kodaly.jpg" alt="Zoltán Kodály" width="330" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kodály_Zoltán_1930s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kodály was born in Kecskemét, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, on 16 December 1882, and learned the violin as a child. In 1900 he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Languages at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> and at the same time Hans von Kössler's composition class at the Royal Hungarian Academy of Music. After his studies he spent a year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> with Charles-Marie Widor.</p><p>In 1905 he visited remote villages to collect songs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phonograph">phonograph</a> cylinders, and in 1906 he wrote a thesis on Hungarian folk song, Strophic Construction in Hungarian Folksong. About then he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-bartok">Béla Bartók</a>, whom he took under his wing and introduced to some of the methods of folk-song collecting. The two became lifelong friends and champions of each other's music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1919 Kodály was appointed alongside Bartók to the Music Directory by the People's Commissariat for Education and Culture in the short-lived Hungarian Soviet Republic. His works blend western European styles, including classical, late-romantic, impressionist and modernist traditions, with the folk music of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and of the Hungarian-inhabited areas of what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><p>Partly because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">Great War</a> and the geopolitical changes that followed, and partly because of a diffident temperament in youth, he had no major public success until 1923. In that year Psalmus Hungaricus was first performed at a concert for the fiftieth anniversary of the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and Pest, at which Bartók's Dance Suite also had its premiere.</p><p>His later works include the stage work Háry János (1926) and its suite, the Dances of Marosszék (1929), the Dances of Galánta (1933), the Variations on a Hungarian folk song, The Peacock (1939), the Concerto for Orchestra (1940) and the Symphony in memoriam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-toscanini">Toscanini</a> (1961).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Beginning in 1935 Kodály worked with his colleague Jenő Ádám on a project to reform music teaching in Hungarian schools. He wrote down no comprehensive method but set out principles: music is for everyone, teaching should be sequential and begin with the child, singing is the first and most valuable tool, and teachers should draw on quality folk songs in the pupils' mother tongue. These principles were widely taken up after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> but also in many other countries. Kodály died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> on 6 March 1967, at the age of 84. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pecs">Pécs</a> commissioned a bronze statue of him by Imre Varga in 1976.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zoltán Kodály” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1950s the communist leader Mátyás Rákosi planned to replace it with an anthem better suited to communist ideology, but the poet Gyula Illyés and the composer <b>Zoltán Kodály</b>, whom he had in mind for the task, both refused.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himnusz">Himnusz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kecskemét, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.91,19.69&amp;year=1937" aria-label="Kecskemét on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zoltán Kodály"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4401 6063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4401 6063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.91,19.69&amp;year=1937">What was here in 1937? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
